  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 146 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  A potent ridge and associated area of surface high \npressure will become established this week, making it feel as if it \nwere still mid-October. In a classic El Nino pattern, deep troughing \nand strong surface lows over the Pacific have caused continuous \nsouthwest to westerly flow out west, flooding the Great Plains and \nwestern Canada with warm Pacific air. This has caused temperature \nanomalies in the 20-30+ degree range. This airmass will begin to \narrive today aloft, but clouds will likely remain stuck under the \ninversion, so it will likely take until Tuesday to warm up. Some \nundercast is likely today due to a shrinking and lowering moisture \nlayer. Starting Tuesday, highs in the 50s will prevail though 60 \nwill be possible in some of the warmest areas. There will be a \nstrong, persistent subsidence inversion, and it will aided by the \nfact that significantly more surface heat will be lost at night than \nwill be able to be replenished by the sun during the day. \nRadiational cooling will help lows drop into the 20s and 30s, around \nclimatological normals.Temperatures at summit level should reach \nwell into the 40s and freezing levels look to eclipse 10,000 ft by \nlate in the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure will be centered south of our \nregion Thursday night into Friday, then drift northward across our \narea Saturday before sliding east. This will provide the northern \nNew York and Vermont area with mainly dry weather and temperatures \nwarmer than seasonal normals. On Sunday a low pressure system north \nof the Great Lakes will track northeastward, and our region will be \nunder southerly flow ahead of the cold front. Sunday could be the \nwarmest of the period, but will see increasing clouds and chances \nfor showers on Sunday as well. We may have a cold front cross the \narea Sunday and bring some scattered rain showers. But there is also \na possibility with two low pressure centers that the better energy \nassociated with each low will pass to our north and to our south, \nleaving us in between without a lot of precipitation. Still much to \nwork out before we get to the end of next weekend, but at this time \nthis may be our best chance for showers through the period.",
